    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The symbiotic relationships that bacteria form with plant roots have far-reaching effects on plant physiology. Analysis of symbiotic interactions yield valuable information about the impact of bacteria in the rhizosphere, the interface of plant-soil interactions.  Current research has focused on the location and interactions of specific plant and bacterial species to gain insights into effects of their association.  My research uses an engineered integrative and conjugative element (ICE) to label bacteria isolated from the roots of the model plant Arabidopsis thaliana with a green fluorescent protein. Fluorescence imagining is then used to visualize bacterial colonization in the roots of wild type and mutant plants. Using this approach, we found that a soil isolate of Bacillus cereus stably colonizes the root tip of both wild type and mutant A. thaliana.  Although the bacteria do not significantly affect plant growth, this strain may be useful as an engineered probiotic in the future because it stably colonizes of the root tip of A. thaliana.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Osaki, Jackie, Dinneny, José and Long, Sharon. (2019). Getting cereus about roots: Tracking dynamic plant-microbe interactions in the A. thaliana root system. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/bn601vn3314",
            "type": "preferred citation",
